<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Giovanni Giustiniani Longo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1453">1453</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Giovanni Giustiniani Longo (died 1453) was a Genoese soldier from a leading family of Chios who brought some 700 men to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in January 1453. Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-xi-palaiologos">Constantine XI</a> placed him in command of the defence of the land walls during the Ottoman siege. Gravely wounded on 29 May, the day the city fell, he was carried from the walls, which hastened the collapse of the defence, and he died of his wounds soon after.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/giovanni-giustiniani-longo.jpg" alt="Giovanni Giustiniani Longo" width="186" height="271" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carlo1213 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giovanni_Giustiniani.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Giustiniani, born in 1418, belonged to the House of Giustiniani and was probably a native of Chios. That he lived as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> suggests he was not the first-born son of his family. He left no testimony of his reasons for going to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. Reportedly Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-xi-palaiologos">Constantine XI</a> promised him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> over the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lemnos">Lemnos</a> in case of victory.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-xi-palaiologos">Constantine XI</a> gave him the rank of protostrator. Why he in particular was chosen is unclear; one explanation offered is that the emperor distrusted the city's large and discontented Venetian population and preferred a Genoese. Giustiniani trained the recruits, strengthened the fortifications and brought back men who had fled to monasteries to escape military duty. Drawing on the siege of 1422, he concentrated the defence on the outer walls. Some 8,000 to 9,000 people were available to defend the city, and his own post was at the lowest-lying section of the walls, between the gates of St. Romanos and Charisius.</p><p>After Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> arrived on 6 April, Giustiniani led sorties that destroyed Ottoman troops, siege works and artillery, until their cost in lives caused them to be given up. Against the Ottoman guns, which included a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> 6 metres long, he had the walls covered with soft surfaces and the breaches filled with debris, into which the cannonballs sank. On 18 April his men repelled the first assault, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">Janissaries</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> at a narrow breach, after four hours.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In the final assault of 29 May two waves were beaten back before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">Janissaries</a> attacked a line held by no more than 3,000 exhausted troops. The accounts differ on his wound: a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> shot or a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crossbow">crossbow</a> bolt struck his arm, chest or leg. He was taken to his ship and sailed away with his men when the fall of the city became known. He died on 1 June and was buried in the church of San Domenico in the Castle of Chios. The tomb is lost, possibly destroyed in the earthquake of 1881, though descriptions of its inscription survive.</p><p>Sources hostile to the Genoese, above all the Venetian eyewitness Nicolò Barbaro, claim that he was lightly wounded or not at all and left his post out of fear. The charges of cowardice and treason spread so widely that the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> denied them in letters to the chanceries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> and others. The historians Marios Philippides and Walter Hanak rebut Barbaro, citing several other sources that record an injury.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Giovanni Giustiniani” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1453 <b>Giovanni Giustiniani Longo</b> led the defence of the land walls of Constantinople against Mehmed II.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Republic of Genoa · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Genoa, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Chios, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.41,8.93&amp;year=1443" aria-label="Genoa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4764 593 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Giovanni Giustiniani Longo"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5214 863h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5214 863h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.41,8.93&amp;year=1443">What was here in 1443? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
